  <main class="legal">
    <h1>Self-Hosting</h1>
    <p class="updated mono">Run StdOut on your own infrastructure — one Docker Compose file, your data stays local</p>

    <p>The self-hosted edition of StdOut runs entirely on your own infrastructure. Your incident history, infrastructure inventory, and knowledge base never leave your machine. You manage updates on your schedule.</p>

    <p>Self-hosting requires a one-time license available from the <a href="https://seayniclabs.com">Seaynic Labs store</a>. After purchase you'll receive a license key and access to the Docker image on GitHub Container Registry.</p>

    <h2>Requirements</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Docker and Docker Compose (v2)</li>
      <li>A machine with at least 512MB available RAM</li>
      <li>Persistent storage for the database (SQLite)</li>
      <li>Optional: a reverse proxy (nginx, Caddy, Traefik) for HTTPS and custom domain</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Installation</h2>

    <h3>1. Authenticate with GitHub Container Registry</h3>

    <p>The StdOut image is hosted on GHCR. You'll need a GitHub account and a personal access token (classic, <code>read:packages</code> scope) to pull it.</p>

    <pre is:raw><code>echo YOUR_GITHUB_PAT | docker login ghcr.io -u YOUR_GITHUB_USERNAME --password-stdin</code></pre>

    <h3>2. Create a compose file</h3>

    <p>Create a directory for StdOut and add a <code>docker-compose.yml</code>:</p>

    <pre is:raw><code>mkdir ~/stdout && cd ~/stdout</code></pre>

    <pre is:raw><code># docker-compose.yml
services:
  stdout:
    image: ghcr.io/seayniclabs/stdout:latest
    restart: unless-stopped
    ports:
      - "8100:3000"
    environment:
      - TZ=America/Chicago
      - STDOUT_LICENSE_KEY=your-license-key
      - STDOUT_SECRET=a-long-random-string-for-session-signing
      - STDOUT_URL=https://your-domain.com
    volumes:
      - ./data:/app/data</code></pre>

    <p>Replace:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>your-license-key</code> — from your purchase confirmation email</li>
      <li><code>a-long-random-string-for-session-signing</code> — generate with <code>openssl rand -hex 32</code></li>
      <li><code>https://your-domain.com</code> — your public URL (used for OAuth callbacks and email links)</li>
      <li><code>America/Chicago</code> — your timezone</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Start the container</h3>

    <pre is:raw><code>docker compose up -d</code></pre>

    <p>StdOut starts on port 8100. Open <code>http://localhost:8100</code> to create your admin account.</p>

    <h2>Data persistence</h2>

    <p>All data lives in the <code>./data</code> directory (mapped to <code>/app/data</code> inside the container):</p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Path</th><th>Contents</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>./data/stdout.db</code></td><td>SQLite database — incidents, stacks, docs, user accounts</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>./data/backups/</code></td><td>Encrypted backup archives (if backups are enabled)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p><strong>Back up <code>./data/stdout.db</code> before any update.</strong> A single <code>cp</code> is enough — SQLite databases are just files.</p>

    <pre is:raw><code>cp ./data/stdout.db ./data/stdout.db.bak</code></pre>

    <h2>Environment variables</h2>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Required</th><th>Description</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>STDOUT_LICENSE_KEY</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>License key from your purchase</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>STDOUT_SECRET</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Random string for session signing — keep this stable</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>STDOUT_URL</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Public URL of your instance (no trailing slash)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>TZ</code></td><td>Recommended</td><td>Timezone for timestamps (e.g. <code>America/New_York</code>)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>STDOUT_PORT</code></td><td>No</td><td>Internal port to bind (default: <code>3000</code>)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Reverse proxy</h2>

    <p>To serve StdOut over HTTPS with a custom domain, put it behind a reverse proxy. The container listens on port 3000 internally (mapped to 8100 in the example above).</p>

    <h3>nginx example</h3>

    <pre><code set:html={`server {\n    listen 443 ssl;\n    server_name stdout.yourdomain.com;\n\n    ssl_certificate     /etc/ssl/certs/yourdomain.crt;\n    ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/private/yourdomain.key;\n\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass http://localhost:8100;\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;\n    }\n}`}></code></pre>

    <h3>Caddy example</h3>

    <pre><code set:html={`stdout.yourdomain.com {\n    reverse_proxy localhost:8100\n}`}></code></pre>

    <p>Caddy handles TLS automatically via Let's Encrypt.</p>

    <h2>Updating</h2>

    <p>To update to the latest release:</p>

    <pre is:raw><code># 1. Back up your database
cp ./data/stdout.db ./data/stdout.db.bak

# 2. Pull the new image
docker compose pull

# 3. Restart with the new image
docker compose up -d</code></pre>

    <p>StdOut runs database migrations automatically on startup. If a migration fails, the container will exit rather than corrupt your data — restore from backup and report the issue.</p>

    <h2>Connecting the Scanner</h2>

    <p>The Scanner works the same way with self-hosted StdOut — point it at your instance URL instead of <code>app.stdout.run</code>:</p>

    <pre is:raw><code>docker run --rm \
  -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock:ro \
  -v ~/.stdout:/data \
  ghcr.io/charlieseay/stdout-scanner scan \
  --url https://stdout.yourdomain.com \
  --token YOUR_TOKEN</code></pre>

    <p>See the <a href="/docs/scanner">Scanner documentation</a> for the full setup guide.</p>

    <h2>Community knowledge base sync</h2>

    <p>Self-hosted instances can pull the curated community knowledge base — real-world resolutions contributed and sanitized by other StdOut users. Sync runs automatically in the background once configured.</p>

    <p>Enable it in <strong>Settings → Knowledge Base → Community Sync</strong>. Your instance will check for new community docs periodically and add them as read-only entries. You can fork any community doc to make it your own and edit it.</p>

    <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>

    <h3>Container exits immediately</h3>
    <p>Check logs with <code>docker compose logs stdout</code>. Missing required environment variables (<code>STDOUT_LICENSE_KEY</code>, <code>STDOUT_SECRET</code>, <code>STDOUT_URL</code>) will cause an immediate exit with a descriptive error.</p>

    <h3>License key not accepted</h3>
    <p>License validation requires the container to reach <code>api.seayniclabs.com</code>. Check that outbound HTTPS is not blocked by your firewall. If your instance is air-gapped, contact support for an offline license.</p>

    <h3>Database locked error</h3>
    <p>Only one container instance should write to <code>stdout.db</code> at a time. If you're running multiple replicas, they will conflict — StdOut self-hosted is single-instance by design.</p>

    <h3>Session expires on restart</h3>
    <p>Make sure <code>STDOUT_SECRET</code> is stable across restarts. If you regenerate it, all active sessions will be invalidated and users will need to log in again.</p>

